  "query_preview": "..\nFriday: Highs warm back into the 80s on Friday as a zonal flow \npattern sets up over the region. Persistent onshore flow will bring \na push of westerly winds through the Cascade gaps Friday evening \nsimilar to the past couple of evenings.\n\nSaturday (Independence Day): Temperatures will continue climbing \nwith highs rising into the mid 80s to low 90s on Saturday. A \"cold\" \nfrontal passage associated with a trough over British Columbia will \nbring breezy to gusty winds and drier air with humidities falling \ninto the teens to low 20s regionwide. Expect westerly winds to pick \nup Saturday afternoon and evening. The strongest winds will be \nfocused over Central WA including the Cascade foothills, Waterville \nPlateau, southern Okanogan Valley, and Colville Reservation. For \nthese locations, expect sustained wind speeds of 15-25 mph and gusts \nup to 40 mph. The warm, dry, and gusty conditions will bring \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions for the Independence \nDay holiday. Thus, Fire Weather Watches have been issued. Outside of \nCentral WA, the foothills of the Blue Mountains will also see winds \non the stronger side and will be monitored for potential expansion \nof the Fire Weather Watch. For the eastern third of WA and the ID \nPanhandle, winds will also pick up but will remain below critical \nthresholds. Winds will subside for most areas by late Saturday \nnight. Exceptions will be the East Cascade slopes, Western Columbia \nBasin, and Okanogan Valley where winds look to persist into Sunday \nmorning.\n\nSunday through Thursday: Warm and very dry conditions will continue \nthrough much of next week. An amplifying ridge over the region will \nsend high temperatures into the 90s regionwide by Monday and \nTuesday. The next period to monitor will be Tuesday into Wednesday \nwhen a trough headed our way will bring potential for another round \nof dry, gusty winds. /Fewkes",
